[38;5;252m[1m## Findings[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One real fix to the Rust, in [38;5;10msrc/strings.rs[0m. [38;5;10mstbds_stralloc[0m computes [38;5;10m512 << (a->block >> 1)[0m. A caller-supplied arena can carry any [38;5;10mblock[0m in [38;5;10m0..=255[0m, making the shift count exceed 64 — C UB, which x86-64 gcc compiles to a [38;5;10mshl[0m masked mod 64. The Rust was leaving that to the LLVM backend (poison for oversized shifts). It now masks explicitly. [38;5;10merr48[0m exercises all 256 [38;5;10mblock[0m values × 2 lengths in separate child pairs; for [38;5;10mblock ≳ 46[0m the C requests a multi-gigabyte block, gets [38;5;10mNULL[0m, and dereferences it — both builds now fault identically.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Three test-oracle bugs I had to correct, worth noting because they were [3mmy[23m assumptions, not translation defects: [38;5;10mrealloc[0m may return the same address after growing (so pointer identity only means "no-op" on the branch where the C skips the allocator); arena result addresses had to be classified structurally rather than by offset; and [38;5;10mstbds_hash_bytes[0m [1mignores its seed[22m — the C XORs [38;5;10mseed[0m into each SipHash state word twice, so it cancels. Binary-key bucket layouts are consequently seed-invariant, which [38;5;10mrow37a[0m/[38;5;10mrow37d[0m now pin down, while [38;5;10mhash_string[0m genuinely does use the seed ([38;5;10mrow37b[0m/[38;5;10mrow37c[0m).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Ground-truth quirks confirmed and reproduced rather than fixed: [38;5;10mhmput_key[0m publishes [38;5;10mtemp_key[0m only in the forward half of its bucket scan; [38;5;10mhmdel_key[0m tests [38;5;10mmode == 1[0m exactly where everything else tests [38;5;10mmode >= 1[0m, so a relocating delete at [38;5;10mmode >= 2[0m aborts on [38;5;10massert(slot >= 0)[0m; [38;5;10marrfreef(NULL)[0m frees a wild pointer; and [38;5;10marrgrowf[0m never checks its [38;5;10mrealloc[0m result.[0m[0m
